{"id":"fe8ee532-4757-47ad-8858-53dd877c8527","object":"chat.completion","created":1784992147,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case.get('nodes')\\n    required_symbols = case.get('required_symbols')\\n    if nodes is None or required_symbols is None:\\n        raise ValueError('Missing required fields: nodes or required_symbols')\\n    node_dict = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        if not all(k in node for k in ('id','parent','symbol','depth','removable','utility')):\\n            raise ValueError('Node missing required fields')\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if not isinstance(nid, str):\\n            raise ValueError('Node id must be string')\\n        node_dict[nid] = node\\n    # Validate root\\n    if 'root' not in node_dict or node_dict['root']['parent'] is not None:\\n        raise ValueError('Root node missing or has parent')\\n    # Build children\\n    children = {nid: [] for nid in node_dict}\\n    for nid, node in node_dict.items():\\n        pid = node['parent']\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            if pid not in node_dict:\\n                raise ValueError(f'Parent {pid} not found')\\n            children[pid].append(nid)\\n    # Validate required symbols exist\\n    all_symbols = {node['symbol'] for node in node_dict.values()}\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if sym not in all_symbols:\\n            raise ValueError(f'Required symbol {sym} not present in any node')\\n    # Precompute descendants including self\\n    descendants = {}\\n    def compute_descendants(nid):\\n        if nid in descendants:\\n            return descendants[nid]\\n        result = {nid}\\n        for child in children[nid]:\\n            result.update(compute_descendants(child))\\n        descendants[nid] = result\\n        return result\\n    for nid in node_dict:\\n        compute_descendants(nid)\\n    # Retained set\\n    retained = set(node_dict.keys())\\n    removed_order = []\\n    # Sorted nodes: deeper first, then id stable\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(node_dict.keys(), key=lambda nid: (-node_dict[nid]['depth'], nid))\\n    for nid in sorted_nodes:\\n        node = node_dict[nid]\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if node['parent'] is None:\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        # Check retained children\\n        child_retained = [c for c in children[nid] if c in retained]\\n        if child_retained:\\n            continue\\n        # Tentative removal: check all required symbols still represented outside subtree\\n        subtree = descendants[nid]\\n        # Count required symbols in retained minus subtree\\n        symbol_counts = {}\\n        for rid in retained:\\n            if rid not in subtree:\\n                sym = node_dict[rid]['symbol']\\n                symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n        ok = True\\n        for sym in required_symbols:\\n            if sym not in symbol_counts or symbol_counts[sym] == 0:\\n                ok = False\\n                break\\n        if ok:\\n            retained.discard(nid)\\n            removed_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task SE-PE-01. The payload has nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols is a list of required symbols.\n\nWe need to perform grammar-guided node reduction: essentially, we have a tree. The objective is to remove removable nodes (where removable=True) possibly, but we must preserve that all required symbols remain present. Also consider deeper nodes before shallower nodes. Also cannot remove root (root has parent null). Cannot remove a node that has retained children? Actually, \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a node has children that are retained, we cannot remove it. So removal is only for leaf nodes? Wait, \"a node with retained children\": if we remove a node, its children would be orphaned? But the description: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" And \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" Means that if after removal, the node's children would become disconnected (since parent removed), but we are not reattaching children? Actually, the problem likely expects that when a node is removed, its children are also removed (since they are part of its subtree). But the description \"retained children\" might mean that if any child is retained (i.e., not removed), then we cannot remove the parent? But the algorithm: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" Typically in tree reduction (like Perses), we try to remove nodes (subtrees) and check if the resulting tree is still grammar-valid and interesting. Here we have a flag removable indicating if it's allowed to be removed. So we can only consider nodes with removable=True. Then we try to remove them, but we must not remove required symbols. Also note: \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" Means if a node has children that we have decided to keep (not removed), we cannot remove the parent (since that would orphan the children). So removal is only possible if all children are also removed. That implies bottom-up removal: we remove leaves first, then their parents if all children removed.\n\nThe procedure: \n- Build tree from nodes list. nodes have 'parent' which is id of parent, or null for root. \n- Determine children for each node.\n- Keep a set of retained node IDs (initially all nodes).\n- Keep a removed_node_ids list in removal order.\n- We consider nodes in order: deeper depth first (higher depth), then for equal depth, use node ID as stable tie break (alphabetical? It says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So sort by depth descending, then by node id ascending? Typically stable would be alphabetical string comparison. We'll use string sorting.\n- For each node in that order:\n   - If node is not removable, skip.\n   - If node is root (parent null), skip.\n   - If node's symbol is in required_symbols, skip.\n   - If node has any children that are still retained (i.e., any child id is in retained_node_ids), then skip because cannot remove with retained children.\n   - Now, we tentatively remove it: consider that if we remove node, its subtree is gone. Check if all required symbols are still represented in the retained nodes (excluding node and its subtree). But careful: since we are removing node, all descendants are gone. So we need to check that for each required symbol, there exists at least one node (other than those in the removed subtree) that has that symbol. Since we are processing bottom-up, descendants might already be removed. So we just need to check the retained set after removal.\n   - If condition holds, then remove node: remove its id from retained, add to removed_node_ids.\n- After processing all nodes, we have removed_node_ids in order of removal, and retained_node_ids (the remaining). Return sorted retained IDs? The output requires \"retained_node_ids\" (array of strings) and \"removed_node_ids\" (array of strings). The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained should be sorted? The schema doesn't specify order for retained, but \"sorted\" suggests alphabetical order? Usually sorted ascending. For removed, it's the order of removal (the sequence we performed). The problem says \"the actual removal order.\" So we keep the order as we removed them.\n\nConstraints: Not mutate case. Use only standard library.\n\nWe'll implement solve(case) that takes the payload, returns dict with removed_node_ids (list in removal order) and retained_node_ids (sorted list).\n\nEdge cases: If root is the only node? Not possible because there are others. Also check for cycles? Not.\n\nAlso need to validate: \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" So we should check that nodes list, each node has id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility (though utility not used). required_symbols must be list. If any missing, raise ValueError.\n\nAlso \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" Infeasible might be if required_symbols includes root's symbol? root is not removable and preserved. Also if required_symbols includes a symbol not present? Then cannot preserve, so infeasible. So we should check that all required symbols are present in at least one node (including root). If not, raise ValueError.\n\nAlso ensure root exists and has parent null.\n\nNow code: We'll parse case. It's a dict. We'll extract nodes list and required_symbols. Build a dict of node by id. For each node, store attributes. Compute children list for each node. Validate required fields.\n\nThen do sorted list of node IDs by (-depth, id). We'll use tuple: (-node['depth'], node['id']) for sorting.\n\nImplement removal loop with retained set and removed order list.\n\nPotential nuance: When we remove node, its children are already removed because we process bottom-up. But if a node has children that are not removable (e.g., permanent), then we cannot remove parent because it has retained children. That's correct.\n\nAlso note: \"Never remove the root ... or a node with retained children.\" So if node has no children (leaf) and removable, proceed.\n\nCheck required symbols after tentative removal: We need to ensure that for each symbol in required_symbols, there is at least one node in retained (excluding the candidate removal) that has that symbol. Since we might remove a node that is the only node with a required symbol, we should not allow. To check, we can compute a set of symbols present in retained before removal, then subtract the symbol of the node if it's the only one? But easier: we can, before removal, for each required symbol, count how many nodes have that symbol in retained. If count >= 2 or the node's symbol is not required, or if it's required but there is another node with same symbol, then removal is okay. But we need to be careful: the node's descendants might also contain required symbols. So we need to consider that removing node removes all its descendants. So we need to compute the multiset of symbols in the subtree rooted at node. Then check if after removal, the retained set still covers all required symbols. So we can precompute subtree symbol frequencies? But we can do: For each required symbol, check if there is any node in retained that is not in the subtree that has that symbol. Since we are processing bottom-up, we can compute for each node the set of symbols in its subtree (or count). We'll do a DFS to compute subtree symbol counts. But easier: just check after removal by iterating over retained nodes that are not in the subtree? That would be O(N^2) if done naively. But N is small (7 nodes in examples). So fine.\n\nWe'll maintain a set of retained node IDs. Also we need to know the subtree of a node to exclude when checking. We can compute descendants set for each node (list of all IDs in its subtree including itself). Precompute by DFS.\n\nThen for tentative removal of node, we get all IDs in its subtree (including node). Then we check required symbols: for each required symbol, is there any node in retained set minus subtree that has that symbol? If yes for all, then can remove. Else skip.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Validate payload: nodes is list, each node has required keys: id (string), parent (string or None), symbol (string), depth (int), removable (bool), utility (float). required_symbols is list of strings. If any missing, raise ValueError.\n2. Build node_dict: id -> dict.\n3. Validate root exists, parent null.\n4. Build children list for each node. Also build parent references for validation maybe.\n5. Check required symbols: For each sym in required_symbols, there must be at least one node with that symbol. If not, raise ValueError.\n6. Precompute descendants: For each node, recursively compute set of descendant IDs (including itself). Could do iterative post-order.\n7. Initialize retained = set of all node IDs.\n8. Initialize removed = [].\n9. Sort node IDs by (-depth, id) using node_dict for depth.\n10. For each node_id in sorted list:\n    node = node_dict[node_id]\n    if not node['removable']: continue\n    if node['parent'] is None: continue (root)\n    if node['symbol'] in required_symbols: continue? Wait: \"Never remove ... a required symbol\". So if node's symbol is in required_symbols, we skip even if removable? Yes, because removing it would eliminate the required symbol. But what if there are multiple nodes with same required symbol? The description says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" which I interpret as a node whose symbol is required. But that might be too strict if there are multiple nodes with that symbol. However, the phrase \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So if there are multiple nodes with same required symbol, removing one might still leave the required symbol represented. But the instruction says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" It says \"a required symbol\" meaning a node that is a required symbol? Actually \"required symbol\" refers to the symbol name. In the context, the root is a node, required symbol is a symbol name. \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" likely means a node whose symbol is in required_symbols. But I think we need to interpret it as: we should not remove a node if its symbol is required and it's the only one providing that symbol? The phrasing is ambiguous. Looking at typical Perses: required symbols are symbols that must remain in the final tree. So any node with that symbol cannot be removed because removing it would remove that symbol. But if there are multiple nodes with the same required symbol, removing one might still leave the symbol represented by another node. However, the problem says \"Never remove ... a required symbol.\" That seems absolute. To be safe, we'll follow the literal: if a node's symbol is in required_symbols, we never remove it, regardless of multiple occurrences. Because the instruction says \"a required symbol\" as a type of node that must not be removed. In the fixtures, required_symbols includes \"s1\". In the first case, nodes with symbol \"s1\" are n1 (depth1) and n4 (depth2) and n... n1 is not removable, n4 is removable. If we remove n4, does that remove a required symbol? n4 has symbol \"s1\". But the node n1 also has symbol \"s1\". So removing n4 still leaves n1 with symbol \"s1\". But the rule says never remove a required symbol, meaning if the node's symbol is required, it's not allowed to be removed even if others exist. I think the intended behavior is that we cannot remove nodes that would cause a required symbol to disappear, not that we cannot remove any node with a required symbol. However, the text says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" (no mention of uniqueness). But also \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That condition would allow removal if there is another node with the same required symbol. So the \"never remove a required symbol\" might be a stricter condition that overrides? Or maybe \"a required symbol\" refers to the set of nodes that are required? I need to look at typical tree reduction problems. In \"Perses\" syntax-guided reduction, they have \"required tokens\" that must remain, but they treat each occurrence? Actually, Perses often has \"interestingness oracle\" that tests the property. Here the required_symbols are like mandatory non-terminals that must appear. So if there are multiple nodes with same symbol, you can remove some as long as at least one remains. But the explicit instruction \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" The phrase \"a required symbol\" could be interpreted as a node that is marked as required? But there is no such per-node flag. So it must be \"a node whose symbol is one of the required symbols.\" Since the root is also a required symbol (root), it's covered. So we must not remove any node whose symbol is in required_symbols. That would prevent removal of n4 in the fixture because n4's symbol is s1 (required). But then why is n4 removable=True? Possibly the fixture is designed to have nodes that are removable but cannot be removed because of required symbol constraint. In that case, the output would have removed_node_ids empty (except maybe none). Let's check the first case: nodes: root (required), n1 (s1 required, not removable), n2 (s2 removable), n3 (s0 not removable), n4 (s1 removable but symbol required), n5 (s2 not removable), n6 (s0 removable). So only removable nodes: n2, n4, n6. n4's symbol s1 is required, so cannot remove. n2's symbol s2 not required, n6's symbol s0 not required. Now consider removal: depth order: depth2 nodes: n4, n5, n6. n4: symbol required -> skip. n5: not removable. n6: removable, symbol s0 not required, children? leaf (no children), parent n1. n1 is not removed (required symbol and not removable). Check if after removing n6, required symbols: root and s1 still present (root and n1). So can remove n6. Then removed list: [\"n6\"]. Then depth2 done. Depth1 nodes: n1 (not removable), n2 (removable), n3 (not removable). n2: leaf? n2 has child n4 (still retained because we didn't remove n4). So n2 has retained child -> skip. So no more removals. So removed_node_ids = [\"n6\"], retained_node_ids sorted all except n6. That matches the expected? Not given expected, but plausible. If we allowed removal of n4 despite required symbol, we would remove n4 first, then n2 could be removed because its only child n4 is gone, then n6 also. But then required s1 would be missing because n1 is still there? Wait n1 also has symbol s1, so s1 still present. So removal of n4 would be allowed by the \"all required symbols remain represented\" condition. So which interpretation is correct? The instruction \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" is absolute. So we should not remove n4. That yields only n6 removed. If we allowed removal, we would get more removals. The problem says \"use only quantities present in the case payload.\" The fixtures are just examples, not providing expected output. But the description of the task says \"grammar_valid, interestingness_preserved\" etc. Here the required_symbols act as an \"interestingness oracle\"? Actually, the \"interestingness oracle\" is defined by required_symbols? The description says \"preserving an interestingness oracle.\" And \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That check is given. So the condition is that after removal, all required symbols still appear. That implies we can remove nodes with required symbols if other nodes with same symbol exist. But the \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" clause seems redundant if the condition already checks representation. Possibly \"a required symbol\" means a node that is individually required (like root is required, but all nodes with required symbol are not individually required, just the symbol as a whole). I need to reconcile.\n\nLook at the Perses algorithm: they have \"required tokens\" that are tokens that must appear in the output. They do not individually protect nodes; they check the final output. So I think the correct interpretation is that we should not remove a node if its symbol is in required_symbols *and* removing it would cause that symbol to be absent. But the condition already does that via \"all required symbols remain represented\". So the explicit \"never remove a required symbol\" might be a redundant restatement for clarity, meaning \"never remove a node if it would cause a required symbol to be absent\". But then what about \"never remove a node with retained children\"? That is separate.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I'll follow the explicit condition: we will not remove a node if its symbol is in required_symbols, because the instruction says \"Never remove ... a required symbol.\" I'll implement that strictly. However, the root is also a required symbol, but we already exclude root. So we'll skip nodes whose symbol is in required_symbols.\n\nBut then why have n4 as removable? Possibly to test that we don't remove it because it's a required symbol. That is plausible.\n\nWe'll go with that. If the test expects otherwise, it might fail, but given the explicit \"Never remove ... a required symbol\", we should follow.\n\nAlternatively, we could interpret \"required symbol\" as a node that is marked as required? But there is no such flag. So it's the symbol name.\n\nTo be safe, we can implement both: first, check if node's symbol is in required_symbols, if so skip. That is straightforward.\n\nNow implement.\n\nWe'll write solve(case). We'll import nothing.\n\nEdge: If there are no removable nodes, removed empty, retained all sorted.\n\nNow produce output with keys removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids. Ensure removed_node_ids is list in order, retained_node_ids sorted (ascending string order).\n\nNow write the Python code as a string inside JSON with key \"implementation\". Escape as needed.\n\nWe'll present final answer as JSON object with single key \"implementation\"."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2169,"completion_tokens":4719,"total_tokens":6888,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3928},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":121},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_pro